WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:08.188
The encoded windowed Landau ceiling reduction is now proved in
first-order Peano Arithmetic and certified in vanilla ACL2.

00:00:08.711 --> 00:00:16.771
It computes a threshold, proves that the bounded search succeeds,
and returns a rational address with a strictly positive lower signal
bound.

00:00:17.294 --> 00:00:21.888
This is the completed arithmetic theorem that the earlier lessons
were building toward.

00:00:22.411 --> 00:00:27.926
Part Eight stopped at a conditional chain whose model and geometry
data still had to be supplied.

00:00:28.448 --> 00:00:34.061
The new construction supplies those data from the encoded zero
region and its small-value probe.

00:00:34.583 --> 00:00:41.492
We will follow what changed, read the final theorem, and explain the
direct Peano Arithmetic proof given in the manuscript.

00:00:42.015 --> 00:00:51.009
The manuscript derives the arithmetic theorem by ordinary induction
on coded finite computations and supplies its connection to the
classical zeta statement.

00:00:51.532 --> 00:00:56.283
This is Part Nine of Landau Windows ACL Two: the completed
reduction.

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:05.455
The theorem begins with a proposed ceiling: a nonnegative rational
constant times x to a rational exponent.

00:01:05.977 --> 00:01:12.147
The exponent is a fraction between zero and one, with one excluded
for the nonempty region we use.

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:21.155
It also takes a rational region describing where a forbidden zero
would be, and a sufficiently accurate rational small-value probe in
that region.

00:01:21.677 --> 00:01:25.980
From the region and ceiling data it computes a threshold for the
window start.

00:01:26.503 --> 00:01:32.261
Every rational start above that threshold leads to a successful
search and a returned violation.

00:01:32.783 --> 00:01:39.244
The theorem does not assume that the desired ceiling has already
been disproved by an externally supplied witness.

00:01:39.767 --> 00:01:42.979
It proves that its own finite search finds the address.

00:01:43.502 --> 00:01:49.417
And it does not prove the separate ceiling hypothesis used by an
entire Riemann Hypothesis argument.

00:01:51.533 --> 00:01:57.570
The construction places the disk center at real coordinate two
hundred fifty-two and a rational seed height.

00:01:58.092 --> 00:02:04.941
A rational outer cap lies strictly between two hundred fifty-one and
two hundred fifty-two minus the ceiling exponent.

00:02:05.464 --> 00:02:11.039
Reserve a positive rational slack and use the smaller radius: cap
minus slack.

00:02:11.562 --> 00:02:17.102
These are conservative constants in a quantitative construction, not
new kinds of infinity.

00:02:17.624 --> 00:02:23.673
The smaller disk stays strictly in the positive half-plane, where
the native Euler evaluator is defined.

00:02:24.195 --> 00:02:33.286
For a hypothetical zero whose real part exceeds the exponent, an
appropriate cap and a small slack place that zero inside a suitable
region.

00:02:33.809 --> 00:02:38.682
The center height can be chosen rationally, with the margin
absorbing its approximation.

00:02:39.205 --> 00:02:43.035
The actual theorem keeps these parameter restrictions explicit.

00:02:45.167 --> 00:02:48.827
An actual complex zero need not have rational coordinates.

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:56.356
The arithmetic statement therefore uses rational probes with
arbitrarily small residuals in one fixed compact disk.

00:02:56.879 --> 00:03:06.959
For every positive rational error request… there is a rational
offset… whose native Euler sample has magnitude at most that
request.

00:03:07.482 --> 00:03:10.282
The offset may change when the request changes.

00:03:10.805 --> 00:03:18.502
This is an arithmetic statement about codes and finite computations;
it does not require storing an irrational point exactly.

00:03:19.024 --> 00:03:23.533
The displayed predicate checks membership in the disk and the small
native value.

00:03:24.056 --> 00:03:30.189
A single accidental small sample at a coarse request is not the
entire encoded-zero condition.

00:03:30.712 --> 00:03:35.876
The full condition gives probes at whatever finer request the
construction eventually needs.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:42.412
Quantifier order is one of the important changes since the
conditional version.

00:03:42.935 --> 00:03:45.808
First fix the region and ceiling parameters.

00:03:46.330 --> 00:03:53.106
The construction chooses its geometry, a positive sample request,
and a window threshold from those data.

00:03:53.629 --> 00:03:59.302
Only then do we use the encoded-zero hypothesis to obtain a probe at
the required accuracy.

00:03:59.824 --> 00:04:03.655
The threshold does not depend on that probe's rational coordinates.

00:04:04.177 --> 00:04:09.292
This prevents the selection from chasing a moving target as the
probe becomes more accurate.

00:04:09.815 --> 00:04:15.803
The final theorem only needs one probe at an error no larger than
one quarter of the computed request.

00:04:16.326 --> 00:04:21.150
The universal encoded-zero condition supplies that particular finite
witness.

00:04:23.267 --> 00:04:30.091
The native continuation evaluator is built from rational finite sums
with computed cutoffs and internal precisions.

00:04:30.614 --> 00:04:36.989
Its intended complex value is Z of s, equal to s minus one times
zeta of s away from one.

00:04:37.512 --> 00:04:39.512
At one, Z has value one.

00:04:40.035 --> 00:04:44.677
That normalization removes the zeta pole at one and gives an entire
function.

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:52.509
So zeros of Z in our region are zeros of zeta; the normalization
point does not introduce a spurious zero.

00:04:53.032 --> 00:04:59.747
The actual wrapper chooses the cutoff and primitive error, then
evaluates a finite dyadic Euler prefix.

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:04.015
The arithmetic bounds compare its values at different requests.

00:05:04.538 --> 00:05:08.792
The classical interpretation identifies the value it approximates.

00:05:10.933 --> 00:05:15.212
The interpretation does not begin by declaring the evaluator to be
zeta.

00:05:15.735 --> 00:05:22.159
Fix a finite cutoff and compare the native computation with a finite
Dirichlet sum plus an Euler correction.

00:05:22.682 --> 00:05:25.858
The correction compensates for the omitted leading tail.

00:05:26.380 --> 00:05:35.229
The certified fixed-truncation estimate separates the native
request, a cutoff term that decreases with the dyadic level, and
primitive rounding error.

00:05:35.752 --> 00:05:39.533
First let the primitive error shrink while the cutoff stays fixed.

00:05:40.056 --> 00:05:46.844
A fractional-part identity then identifies the limit of those finite
corrected sums as the normalized zeta value.

00:05:47.367 --> 00:05:49.609
Finally let the cutoff increase.

00:05:50.132 --> 00:05:53.865
The result is an error bound of one quarter of the native request.

00:05:54.388 --> 00:06:00.958
The manuscript proves the finite arithmetic bounds and establishes
this zeta comparison in its final section.

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:08.409
The contour construction needs regions where the native Euler value
stays away from zero.

00:06:08.932 --> 00:06:17.041
On the real axis, the proof pairs alternating Dirichlet terms,
controls the Euler correction, and obtains a positive lower bound.

00:06:17.564 --> 00:06:21.927
A quantitative neighborhood argument extends that clearance to a
narrow strip.

00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:28.911
Strictly to the right of one, finite Mobius weights produce an
approximate inverse with a positive lower bound.

00:06:29.433 --> 00:06:34.064
These are arithmetic constructions with finite truncation and
rounding budgets.

00:06:34.586 --> 00:06:39.702
They replace the earlier need to supply those clearances as separate
geometric promises.

00:06:40.224 --> 00:06:45.170
The size of every safe region and the precision needed there are
part of the computed data.

00:06:45.692 --> 00:06:50.226
None of this says that a picture of a zero-free region is itself a
certificate.

00:06:52.367 --> 00:06:59.433
The proof constructs finite polynomial approximations and rational
contour charts with explicit lower value bounds.

00:06:59.956 --> 00:07:05.132
A chart is a collection of local checks whose quantitative radii
cover the required contour.

00:07:05.655 --> 00:07:11.509
The lower bounds must remain compatible when factorization and
approximation precision are refined.

00:07:12.032 --> 00:07:16.262
The new construction derives a uniform floor from its fixed coarse
data.

00:07:16.785 --> 00:07:22.955
That avoids choosing an index from a cap that secretly depends on
the index's later precision request.

00:07:23.477 --> 00:07:29.211
The circles carry finite integer counts, rather than a demand to
name an exact analytic zero.

00:07:29.733 --> 00:07:35.236
Accepted charts and count comparisons can therefore be manipulated
by rational arithmetic.

00:07:35.759 --> 00:07:41.505
The pictures here illustrate the interfaces; the computed objects in
the proof are much larger.

00:07:43.633 --> 00:07:47.488
Begin with a certified empty circle centered on the real axis.

00:07:48.011 --> 00:07:52.592
Scan a finite sequence of real-centered circles toward the desired
outer cap.

00:07:53.115 --> 00:07:54.606
There are two outcomes.

00:07:55.129 --> 00:08:01.759
If the scan reaches an outer empty circle, the original small probe
supplies the positive modulated count.

00:08:02.282 --> 00:08:07.955
If it encounters a positive count, use the first positive successor
of the preceding empty circle.

00:08:08.477 --> 00:08:13.265
A computed interior probe from that positive circle supplies the
modulation height.

00:08:13.788 --> 00:08:19.497
The real-axis and right-half-plane clearances leave enough radial
room for the required nesting.

00:08:20.020 --> 00:08:27.911
Both cases produce the same checked pair interface: an empty real
circle and a compatible modulated circle with positive count.

00:08:30.033 --> 00:08:33.791
The earlier isolated-pole picture made one point look special.

00:08:34.314 --> 00:08:40.726
The completed reduction does not assume that every hypothetical zero
is simple or separated from its neighbors.

00:08:41.248 --> 00:08:44.061
It uses small values and finite counts.

00:08:44.583 --> 00:08:51.698
Positive count gives a sufficiently accurate interior probe; a small
enough probe prevents the relevant count from being zero.

00:08:52.221 --> 00:08:58.318
Several zeros at one location or several nearby distinct locations
enter through that same interface.

00:08:58.841 --> 00:09:02.938
The construction still proves its own clearances and contour
validity.

00:09:03.461 --> 00:09:08.588
What disappears is the requirement to provide an exact isolated zero
in advance.

00:09:09.111 --> 00:09:16.092
This is why the cluster work was a mathematical part of the
reduction, rather than a cosmetic addition to the diagram.

00:09:18.233 --> 00:09:22.197
The source card shows the constructor that packages the circle data.

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:25.629
It first obtains the real-circle scan result.

00:09:26.152 --> 00:09:32.891
If a positive successor was found, it computes a modulation height
from that result; otherwise it keeps the seed height.

00:09:33.414 --> 00:09:38.977
It builds the modulated cover and selects the positive sample
request appropriate to the case.

00:09:39.500 --> 00:09:45.694
The returned list records the chosen height, both covers, an
interior real radius, and the request.

00:09:46.217 --> 00:09:49.829
The short request wrapper extracts the last of those fields.

00:09:50.352 --> 00:09:56.533
This is the data previously supplied to the conditional theorem, now
generated by the arithmetic construction.

00:09:57.056 --> 00:10:02.244
Its validity theorems connect the returned fields to the checks
needed by the coefficient argument.

00:10:04.367 --> 00:10:08.597
Part Seven computed oscillating Mellin samples and their finite
sums.

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:17.702
The completed proof connects the actual improper evaluator to the
native Euler coefficient family, rather than accepting an unrelated
analytic model.

00:10:18.224 --> 00:10:22.139
The coefficients are taken in the shifted variable s minus z.

00:10:22.662 --> 00:10:27.777
That orientation accounts for the reciprocal-pole coefficient
convention used in Part Eight.

00:10:28.300 --> 00:10:35.694
The comparison keeps independent precision requests on both
computations and proves an explicit disk allowance for their
difference.

00:10:36.217 --> 00:10:40.520
A common refinement lets the proof make that allowance as small as
needed.

00:10:41.042 --> 00:10:47.891
The zero data, contour data, and coefficients now refer to
compatible constructions in the same arithmetic chain.

00:10:48.414 --> 00:10:54.353
This is the connection needed before a coefficient contradiction can
say anything about the real window search.

00:10:56.500 --> 00:11:01.967
The completed proof also retains the plain height-zero reading from
the one-sided inequality.

00:11:02.489 --> 00:11:08.550
Failure of the window search bounds the magnitude of an oscillating
coefficient plus that real coefficient.

00:11:09.073 --> 00:11:13.897
The coefficient at height zero can be negative, so dropping it would
change the statement.

00:11:14.420 --> 00:11:19.026
The empty real-circle construction supplies the separate control
needed for it.

00:11:19.548 --> 00:11:26.009
The selected-height and real-height models are generated together so
their normalizations and radii are compatible.

00:11:26.532 --> 00:11:31.114
After adding the anchor allowance, search failure gives the required
upper cap.

00:11:31.636 --> 00:11:36.194
The large-index coefficient argument supplies the conflicting lower
information.

00:11:36.717 --> 00:11:42.438
The conclusion depends on that combined estimate, just as the small
three-value example did.

00:11:44.567 --> 00:11:53.100
There are several error channels: finite evaluation, contour
approximation, coefficient comparison, and geometric remainder
terms.

00:11:53.623 --> 00:11:57.902
The proof assigns explicit rational portions of the final budget to
them.

00:11:58.424 --> 00:12:07.370
More accurate factor data can change the lists and their degree, so
a cap chosen from an arbitrary later approximation would risk a
circular dependency.

00:12:07.892 --> 00:12:14.050
The completed construction instead derives the needed uniform caps
and clearances from fixed coarse data.

00:12:14.573 --> 00:12:20.536
Then it chooses an index large enough for the geometric terms and
requests the required finer precision.

00:12:21.059 --> 00:12:24.962
This order lets the same proof work at every subsequent refinement.

00:12:25.485 --> 00:12:32.648
The theorem is about the whole prescribed parameter range, not a
successful numerical experiment at one convenient input.

00:12:33.171 --> 00:12:39.777
The thresholds are conservative; the result promises a certified
witness, not a practical running time.

00:12:41.900 --> 00:12:45.500
The threshold wrapper first calls the paired-circle constructor.

00:12:46.023 --> 00:12:53.211
It reads the chosen height, the modulated cover, the empty cover,
and the reserved real radius from the returned data.

00:12:53.733 --> 00:12:58.970
It then calls the proved pair-threshold routine with those fields
and the ceiling parameters.

00:12:59.492 --> 00:13:04.777
The small-value request and the window threshold use the same
prepared region data.

00:13:05.300 --> 00:13:11.700
The source makes their independence from the later offset visible:
the offset is not an input to either wrapper.

00:13:12.223 --> 00:13:17.750
The validity theorems prove that the request is positive and the
threshold is a positive integer.

00:13:18.273 --> 00:13:24.418
At this point the infinite-looking Landau argument has supplied the
finite bounds needed to run the window search.

00:13:24.941 --> 00:13:28.820
We can now read the final theorem as an input-and-output contract.

00:13:30.933 --> 00:13:37.467
The first source page lists the rational region, slack, ceiling,
exponent, and window-start hypotheses.

00:13:37.989 --> 00:13:44.547
It also requires a passing approximate-zero predicate at a request
at most one quarter of the prepared request.

00:13:45.070 --> 00:13:49.555
The conclusion runs the actual bounded window search and computes
its address.

00:13:50.077 --> 00:13:57.459
It says the search result exists, the address is rational, and the
address satisfies the two window inequalities.

00:13:57.982 --> 00:14:06.200
It also says that the returned lower signal endpoint is strictly
positive and that the interval is exactly the one produced by the
signal evaluator.

00:14:06.723 --> 00:14:12.202
The final page contains the proof's use of the preceding reduction
and probe-admission theorems.

00:14:12.724 --> 00:14:19.839
These proof hints direct ACL2 toward already checked results; they
are not extra assumptions added to the theorem.

00:14:20.362 --> 00:14:23.744
The complete source form is shown across the numbered cards.

00:14:24.267 --> 00:14:27.527
The name of this result is the encoded-zero reduction.

00:14:29.667 --> 00:14:33.679
The theorem uses the same finite search built earlier in the series.

00:14:34.202 --> 00:14:40.347
The search wrapper chooses the constructed grid and evaluates the
centered signal minus the proposed ceiling.

00:14:40.870 --> 00:14:46.021
The address wrapper converts a returned sample index into a rational
point on that grid.

00:14:46.544 --> 00:14:53.974
For a window beginning at Y, the conclusion gives Y less than or
equal to x and x squared less than or equal to Y cubed.

00:14:54.497 --> 00:15:00.667
Because the quantities are nonnegative, this places x in the window
from Y to Y to the power three halves.

00:15:01.189 --> 00:15:07.032
The squared comparison is an exact rational test; no rounded square
root defines the endpoint.

00:15:07.555 --> 00:15:13.761
For illustration, a window beginning at four ends at eight, and the
rational address six lies inside it.

00:15:14.283 --> 00:15:20.889
Those small numbers illustrate the conclusion's geometry, not an
execution of the enormous final threshold.

00:15:23.033 --> 00:15:29.482
The returned interval encloses Gz of x minus the ceiling constant
times x to the chosen exponent.

00:15:30.005 --> 00:15:37.811
The prime-power classifier, logarithm bands, Euler-constant bands,
and rational-power bands provide the components of that enclosure.

00:15:38.333 --> 00:15:43.982
The interval operations preserve their real meanings through finite
addition, scaling and subtraction.

00:15:44.505 --> 00:15:50.092
So a strictly positive lower endpoint proves a strict ceiling
violation at the returned address.

00:15:50.615 --> 00:15:55.088
It is stronger than a positive floating-point estimate with no error
allowance.

00:15:55.611 --> 00:16:01.817
If the lower endpoint were one eighth in an illustrative enclosure,
the true difference would be at least one eighth.

00:16:02.339 --> 00:16:08.073
The theorem computes the actual endpoint; the film is not
substituting that illustrative value.

00:16:08.595 --> 00:16:14.777
The interpretation document spells out the original smoothed prime
statistic that the evaluator encloses.

00:16:16.900 --> 00:16:24.197
Return to the encoded-zero condition: every positive rational
request has a suitable rational probe in the fixed region.

00:16:24.720 --> 00:16:29.702
The arithmetic construction proves that one particular positive
request is sufficient.

00:16:30.224 --> 00:16:34.588
Instantiate the encoded-zero condition at one quarter of that
request.

00:16:35.111 --> 00:16:40.395
The final theorem now applies to every rational window start above
its computed threshold.

00:16:40.918 --> 00:16:47.767
There is no need to choose a new analytic zero, guess a contour, or
supply a separate peak selector for each window.

00:16:48.289 --> 00:16:50.883
The search returns the violation directly.

00:16:51.406 --> 00:17:00.836
In contrapositive form, a failed search above the threshold excludes
every probe at that required accuracy, and therefore excludes the
encoded-zero condition.

00:17:01.359 --> 00:17:05.080
That is the effective arithmetic ceiling-to-zero-free conversion.

00:17:07.200 --> 00:17:13.661
The Peano Arithmetic proof represents fractions, lists, and complex
pairs by natural-number codes.

00:17:14.183 --> 00:17:17.371
Represent an integer by a sign and a magnitude.

00:17:17.894 --> 00:17:24.173
Represent a rational by a numerator and a nonzero denominator, with
the chosen normalization convention.

00:17:24.695 --> 00:17:29.471
Finite pairs and lists can themselves be represented by natural-
number codes.

00:17:29.994 --> 00:17:37.982
A rational complex value is then a code for two rational
coordinates, and a polynomial is a code for a finite coefficient
list.

00:17:38.505 --> 00:17:43.232
The graphs of the recursive functions become arithmetic relations on
those codes.

00:17:43.755 --> 00:17:50.906
This is how the theorem can be stated using whole-number variables
even though its convenient working notation uses richer data.

00:17:51.429 --> 00:17:59.647
The encoding must preserve the operations and hypotheses used by the
proof; merely renaming a fraction as an integer would not suffice.

00:18:01.767 --> 00:18:09.136
We now have a direct mathematical proof in first-order Peano
Arithmetic, as well as the machine-checked ACL2 development.

00:18:09.659 --> 00:18:17.053
The manuscript proves the encoded windowed ceiling theorem by
deriving its finite computations and inequalities in arithmetic.

00:18:17.576 --> 00:18:24.630
Natural-number codes represent integers, rationals, finite lists,
polynomials, and computation graphs.

00:18:25.153 --> 00:18:31.080
Termination and induction are justified using natural-valued
measures and ordinary strong induction.

00:18:31.603 --> 00:18:38.391
Section fourteen supplies the finite Mellin quadrature estimates
rather than importing an unproved analytic estimate.

00:18:38.914 --> 00:18:45.956
Theorem fifteen point two assembles the contour construction,
coefficient bounds, threshold, and returned rational witness.

00:18:46.479 --> 00:18:51.606
The manuscript also proves the correspondence with the classical
zeta-function statement.

00:18:52.129 --> 00:19:01.268
The result is proved in Peano Arithmetic; the manuscript and ACL2
certificates provide the direct arithmetic proof and the machine-
checked development.

00:19:03.400 --> 00:19:11.485
The release checkpoint contains six hundred forty-seven certified
ACL2 books and three thousand six hundred forty-nine exported
theorems.

00:19:12.008 --> 00:19:15.874
The certificate files are present and newer than their source files.

00:19:16.397 --> 00:19:21.961
Loading the final book successfully imports its five hundred fifty-
eight-book dependency closure.

00:19:22.483 --> 00:19:30.011
The source audit found no skipped-proof, custom-axiom, trust-tag, or
function-attachment events in the recorded books.

00:19:30.533 --> 00:19:34.473
The source hashes identify the exact checkpoint behind this film.

00:19:34.995 --> 00:19:42.050
These checks support reproducibility and reveal the proof path; they
do not replace understanding the theorem statement.

00:19:42.573 --> 00:19:50.124
The direct Peano Arithmetic manuscript and its classical comparison
are recorded alongside the ACL2 certificate audit.

00:19:50.647 --> 00:19:55.423
The proof source is available for independent checking with vanilla
ACL2.

00:19:57.567 --> 00:20:05.445
The classical comparison proves that the native Euler value differs
from the normalized zeta value by at most one quarter of its
request.

00:20:05.968 --> 00:20:13.762
Continuity and rational density turn an actual zero in the interior
of a suitable disk into arbitrarily fine rational probes.

00:20:14.285 --> 00:20:21.970
Conversely, probes in one compact disk with errors tending to zero
have a limit point where the normalized zeta value vanishes.

00:20:22.492 --> 00:20:28.153
The value at one is one, so that limit does not manufacture a zero
at the normalization point.

00:20:28.676 --> 00:20:34.858
The signal enclosure turns the returned positive endpoint into a
genuine violation of the classical ceiling.

00:20:35.380 --> 00:20:42.350
Rational approximation of exponents, heights, constants, and window
starts then gives the recurring-window formulation.

00:20:42.873 --> 00:20:47.685
The manuscript proves this comparison after completing the
arithmetic derivation.

00:20:48.208 --> 00:20:56.486
Both are supplied: the Peano Arithmetic proof of the encoded
reduction, and its mathematical identification with the original
Landau statement.

00:20:58.633 --> 00:21:07.227
The encoded windowed Landau ceiling reduction is proved in Peano
Arithmetic, with the corresponding development certified in vanilla
ACL2.

00:21:07.750 --> 00:21:15.956
It supplies a computed threshold, a proof of search success, and a
certified rational witness under the encoded zero hypotheses.

00:21:16.479 --> 00:21:25.848
The contour and budget constructors discharge the applicability
inputs left open in Part Eight, and the manuscript derives their
arithmetic guarantees directly.

00:21:26.371 --> 00:21:31.765
The result handles multiple and clustered zeros through the small-
value and count interface.

00:21:32.288 --> 00:21:37.258
This is one essential conversion in a larger Riemann Hypothesis
proof architecture.

00:21:37.780 --> 00:21:48.302
It does not establish the separate ceiling hypothesis, prove every
component of that architecture in Peano Arithmetic, or claim a full
arithmetic proof of the Riemann Hypothesis.

00:21:48.824 --> 00:21:51.855
Those are different questions about further reductions.

00:21:52.377 --> 00:21:59.783
The value of this result is that this particular bridge now has a
concrete arithmetic proof path with its interpretation exposed.

00:22:01.900 --> 00:22:11.124
The repository now connects the Peano Arithmetic manuscript, the
final ACL2 theorem, the certificate inventory, and the proof source
hashes.

00:22:11.647 --> 00:22:17.671
The companion lesson book puts the videos beside worked articles and
interactive arithmetic examples.

00:22:18.194 --> 00:22:24.036
Those demos help explain the constructions; they are not substitutes
for the ACL2 checker.

00:22:24.559 --> 00:22:31.262
To reproduce the machine-checked development, use the pinned local
toolchain instructions and the certification command.

00:22:31.785 --> 00:22:39.397
To read the Peano Arithmetic proof, begin with Theorem fifteen point
two and its preceding finite derivations in the manuscript.

00:22:39.920 --> 00:22:44.392
We started with successor, induction, and a finite sum of odd
numbers.

00:22:44.915 --> 00:22:49.885
We finish with a finite proof that a prescribed search must return a
